K-quant MMQ on RDNA2 / RDNA3: 16-byte activation loads from aligned shared memory ("wide LDS")

From llama.cpp-rdna2 (e194cbddc), ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmq.cuh and mmq-vec-dot.cuh: the Q4_K / Q5_K / Q6_K dot bodies
copy their 64-byte q8_1 activation window out of shared memory with four 16-byte loads into a local array, and the
kernel's shared memory is declared int4 (16-byte aligned), so the compiler emits ds_read_b128 instead of scalar loads.
Same integers reach the same dot products: the output is bit for bit the same.

Compiled only into a template instance whose source file is built with the macro of its type
(GGML_HIP_RDNA2_Q4_K_MMQ_WIDE_LDS=1 on template-instances/mmq-instance-q4_k.cu, ..._Q5_K_..., ..._Q6_K_...; the RDNA3
names for gfx11).  llama.cpp-rdna2 sets them in ggml/src/ggml-hip/CMakeLists.txt; Strata's CMakeLists.txt sets the
RDNA2 ones (STRATA_MMQ_WIDE_LDS).  Without the macro every instance compiles to the code it had.

Two things here are not in llama.cpp-rdna2:
  - GGML_HIP_RDNA_WIDE_LDS_MIN_J (32): tiles narrower than that keep the stock declaration and loads.  On gfx1030 the
    aligned ones at every J were 2-4% slower at J = 16 (Q5_K, Q6_K) and no faster at J = 8 and 24.  Q5_K's window
    stays at J >= 64, as there (measured here too: at J < 64 it loses to the aligned declaration alone).
  - ggml_hip_mmq_wide_lds_switches: the switches change device code only, and ccache keys a HIP object on the host
    pass's preprocessed text, so it handed back the object of the other setting.  The constant puts the setting into
    that text.

Base: llama.cpp 3cf03257f219afbe7334045ff7c6a06ac68c627d with 0001 applied or not (no overlap).
Apply:  patch -p1 -d third_party/llama.cpp < third_party/llama.cpp-patches/0002-kquant-mmq-rdna-wide-lds.patch
Remove: the same with -R, or build with -DSTRATA_MMQ_WIDE_LDS=OFF.

